    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "numCourses = 2, prerequisites = [[1,0]]         ->  true    (take 0, then 1)\nnumCourses = 2, prerequisites = [[1,0],[0,1]]   ->  false   (each needs the other first)\nnumCourses = 4, prerequisites = [[1,0],[2,0],[3,1],[3,2]]   ->  true"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: topological sort"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Whenever a problem says *prerequisites*, *dependencies*, *build order* or *A must come before B*, draw an **arrow** from each thing to whatever depends on it. You get a directed graph, and the question becomes: can every vertex be lined up so that all arrows point forwards? Such a line-up is a **topological order**."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "It exists exactly when the graph has **no directed cycle**. A cycle is a set of tasks that each wait for another one in the set, so none of them can ever go first."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Kahn's algorithm** builds the order the way you'd plan a timetable. Count, for every vertex, how many arrows point into it — its **in-degree**, the prerequisites it still needs. Anything at 0 can be done now. Doing it removes its outgoing arrows, which may bring other vertices down to 0. Repeat. If you run out of ready vertices before everything is done, the leftovers are stuck behind a cycle."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: keep scanning for a ready course"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Go through the courses again and again, taking any course whose prerequisites have all been taken. Stop when a full pass takes nothing new."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function canFinish(numCourses, prerequisites) {\n  const taken = new Array(numCourses).fill(false);\n  let count = 0;\n  let progress = true;\n  while (progress) {\n    progress = false;\n    for (let c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n      if (taken[c]) continue;\n      // c is ready when every one of its prerequisites is already taken\n      const ready = prerequisites.every(([course, pre]) => course !== c || taken[pre]);\n      if (ready) {\n        taken[c] = true;\n        count++;\n        progress = true;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return count === numCourses;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def can_finish(num_courses, prerequisites):\n    taken = [False] * num_courses\n    count = 0\n    progress = True\n    while progress:\n        progress = False\n        for c in range(num_courses):\n            if taken[c]:\n                continue\n            # c is ready when every one of its prerequisites is already taken\n            if all(course != c or taken[pre] for course, pre in prerequisites):\n                taken[c] = True\n                count += 1\n                progress = True\n    return count == num_courses"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {\n    boolean[] taken = new boolean[numCourses];\n    int count = 0;\n    boolean progress = true;\n    while (progress) {\n        progress = false;\n        for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n            if (taken[c]) continue;\n            // c is ready when every one of its prerequisites is already taken\n            boolean ready = true;\n            for (int[] p : prerequisites) {\n                if (p[0] == c && !taken[p[1]]) {\n                    ready = false;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if (ready) {\n                taken[c] = true;\n                count++;\n                progress = true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return count == numCourses;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool canFinish(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    vector<bool> taken(numCourses, false);\n    int count = 0;\n    bool progress = true;\n    while (progress) {\n        progress = false;\n        for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n            if (taken[c]) continue;\n            // c is ready when every one of its prerequisites is already taken\n            bool ready = true;\n            for (auto& p : prerequisites) {\n                if (p[0] == c && !taken[p[1]]) {\n                    ready = false;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if (ready) {\n                taken[c] = true;\n                count++;\n                progress = true;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return count == numCourses;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V² · E), space O(V).** A pass checks every course against every prerequisite pair, and a long chain of courses needs up to V passes. The waste: each pass re-checks courses that are nowhere near ready."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Kahn's algorithm: BFS on in-degrees"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Build the arrows once, and keep a count of what each course is still waiting for. A queue holds the courses that are ready. Taking a course lowers the count of every course it unlocks; one that reaches 0 joins the queue."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "course-schedule",
      "caption": "Courses start when their count of unfinished prerequisites hits 0. Try the cycle example."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function canFinish(numCourses, prerequisites) {\n  const next = Array.from({ length: numCourses }, () => []);   // course -> courses it unlocks\n  const indegree = new Array(numCourses).fill(0);              // prerequisites still to take\n  for (const [course, pre] of prerequisites) {\n    next[pre].push(course);\n    indegree[course]++;\n  }\n  const queue = [];\n  for (let c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n    if (indegree[c] === 0) queue.push(c);   // nothing to wait for: ready now\n  }\n  let taken = 0;\n  let head = 0;\n  while (head < queue.length) {\n    const c = queue[head++];\n    taken++;                                // take course c\n    for (const d of next[c]) {\n      indegree[d]--;                        // one prerequisite of d is done\n      if (indegree[d] === 0) queue.push(d); // all of d's prerequisites are done: d is ready\n    }\n  }\n  return taken === numCourses;              // a course never taken is stuck on a cycle\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from collections import deque\n\ndef can_finish(num_courses, prerequisites):\n    nxt = [[] for _ in range(num_courses)]     # course -> courses it unlocks\n    indegree = [0] * num_courses               # prerequisites still to take\n    for course, pre in prerequisites:\n        nxt[pre].append(course)\n        indegree[course] += 1\n    queue = deque(c for c in range(num_courses) if indegree[c] == 0)   # ready now\n    taken = 0\n    while queue:\n        c = queue.popleft()\n        taken += 1                             # take course c\n        for d in nxt[c]:\n            indegree[d] -= 1                   # one prerequisite of d is done\n            if indegree[d] == 0:\n                queue.append(d)                # d is ready\n    return taken == num_courses                # a course never taken is stuck on a cycle"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {\n    List<List<Integer>> next = new ArrayList<>();       // course -> courses it unlocks\n    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) next.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    int[] indegree = new int[numCourses];               // prerequisites still to take\n    for (int[] p : prerequisites) {\n        next.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);\n        indegree[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    ArrayDeque<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n        if (indegree[c] == 0) queue.add(c);             // nothing to wait for: ready now\n    }\n    int taken = 0;\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        int c = queue.poll();\n        taken++;                                        // take course c\n        for (int d : next.get(c)) {\n            if (--indegree[d] == 0) queue.add(d);       // all of d's prerequisites are done\n        }\n    }\n    return taken == numCourses;                         // a course never taken is stuck on a cycle\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool canFinish(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    vector<vector<int>> next(numCourses);               // course -> courses it unlocks\n    vector<int> indegree(numCourses, 0);                // prerequisites still to take\n    for (auto& p : prerequisites) {\n        next[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);\n        indegree[p[0]]++;\n    }\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n        if (indegree[c] == 0) q.push(c);                // nothing to wait for: ready now\n    }\n    int taken = 0;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int c = q.front(); q.pop();\n        taken++;                                        // take course c\n        for (int d : next[c]) {\n            if (--indegree[d] == 0) q.push(d);          // all of d's prerequisites are done\n        }\n    }\n    return taken == numCourses;                         // a course never taken is stuck on a cycle\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V + E), space O(V + E).** Each course enters the queue at most once, and each arrow lowers a count exactly once. The arrow lists take O(V + E)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. DFS with three states"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A cycle check on its own also answers the question. In a **directed** graph, *visited* isn't enough: reaching a finished course a second way (two courses that share a prerequisite) is not a loop. So a DFS keeps three states — **new**, **on the current path**, **done** — and only an arrow back onto the *current path* is a cycle."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function canFinish(numCourses, prerequisites) {\n  const next = Array.from({ length: numCourses }, () => []);\n  for (const [course, pre] of prerequisites) next[pre].push(course);\n  const state = new Array(numCourses).fill(0);   // 0 = new, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n  const hasCycle = (c) => {\n    if (state[c] === 1) return true;             // back onto the current path: a cycle\n    if (state[c] === 2) return false;            // already checked: no cycle from here\n    state[c] = 1;\n    for (const d of next[c]) {\n      if (hasCycle(d)) return true;\n    }\n    state[c] = 2;\n    return false;\n  };\n  for (let c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n    if (hasCycle(c)) return false;\n  }\n  return true;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def can_finish(num_courses, prerequisites):\n    nxt = [[] for _ in range(num_courses)]\n    for course, pre in prerequisites:\n        nxt[pre].append(course)\n    state = [0] * num_courses                  # 0 = new, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n\n    def has_cycle(c):\n        if state[c] == 1:\n            return True                        # back onto the current path: a cycle\n        if state[c] == 2:\n            return False                       # already checked: no cycle from here\n        state[c] = 1\n        if any(has_cycle(d) for d in nxt[c]):\n            return True\n        state[c] = 2\n        return False\n\n    return not any(has_cycle(c) for c in range(num_courses))"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {\n    List<List<Integer>> next = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) next.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (int[] p : prerequisites) next.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);\n    int[] state = new int[numCourses];                  // 0 = new, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n    for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n        if (hasCycle(next, state, c)) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nboolean hasCycle(List<List<Integer>> next, int[] state, int c) {\n    if (state[c] == 1) return true;                     // back onto the current path: a cycle\n    if (state[c] == 2) return false;                    // already checked: no cycle from here\n    state[c] = 1;\n    for (int d : next.get(c)) {\n        if (hasCycle(next, state, d)) return true;\n    }\n    state[c] = 2;\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool hasCycle(vector<vector<int>>& next, vector<int>& state, int c) {\n    if (state[c] == 1) return true;                     // back onto the current path: a cycle\n    if (state[c] == 2) return false;                    // already checked: no cycle from here\n    state[c] = 1;\n    for (int d : next[c]) {\n        if (hasCycle(next, state, d)) return true;\n    }\n    state[c] = 2;\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool canFinish(int numCourses, vector<vector<int>>& prerequisites) {\n    vector<vector<int>> next(numCourses);\n    for (auto& p : prerequisites) next[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);\n    vector<int> state(numCourses, 0);                   // 0 = new, 1 = on the current path, 2 = done\n    for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) {\n        if (hasCycle(next, state, c)) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V + E), space O(V + E).** A course is expanded once — after that it's *done* and returns at once. The recursion can be V calls deep on a long chain of prerequisites."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: keep scanning for a ready course",
          "time": "O(V² · E)",
          "timeWhy": "Each pass checks courses against all **E** pairs, and a long chain needs up to **V** passes.",
          "space": "O(V)",
          "spaceWhy": "A done mark **per course**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Kahn's algorithm: BFS on in-degrees",
          "time": "O(V + E)",
          "timeWhy": "Each course enters the queue **once**; each arrow lowers a count **once**.",
          "space": "O(V + E)",
          "spaceWhy": "The arrow lists and **V** in-degree counts."
        },
        {
          "approach": "DFS with three states",
          "time": "O(V + E)",
          "timeWhy": "Each course is expanded **once**; after that it's done and returns at once.",
          "space": "O(V + E)",
          "spaceWhy": "The arrow lists, plus recursion up to **V** deep."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Kahn's and the three-state DFS are equally fast. Kahn's has no recursion and hands you the order directly, which the next lesson needs; the DFS is the classic way to *find* a cycle."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**No prerequisites** — every course is ready at once: `true`.",
        "**Two courses that need each other** — the smallest cycle: `false`.",
        "**A course that is its own prerequisite** — `[0, 0]` is a cycle of length one.",
        "**A diamond** — 1 and 2 both need 0, and 3 needs both. Course 3 is reached twice, which is fine: it's not a cycle. A plain *visited* check gets this wrong."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Course Schedule II** — return an actual order. Next lesson.",
        "**Parallel Courses** — the fewest semesters if you can take any number of ready courses at once: run Kahn's level by level and count the levels.",
        "**Find Eventual Safe States** — vertices that can't reach a cycle: Kahn's on the reversed arrows, or the same three-state DFS."
      ]
    }
  ],
